of how they're doing things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.440 --> 00:15:37.800
and these are their suggestions for trying to do&nbsp;
things a little bit faster without sacrificing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:37.800 --> 00:15:43.240
the quality. And the client typically leaves&nbsp;
it in our hands to make the decisions. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.240 --> 00:15:48.960
just kind of let us know the criteria before an&nbsp;
order starts. But, yeah, that's my two cents.

00:15:48.960 --> 00:15:55.120
Okay, good stuff, Greg. So, basically, due&nbsp;
diligence, your factory selection, sampling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.120 --> 00:15:58.920
you get negotiation order confirmation,&nbsp;
and obviously, the quality control.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.560 --> 00:16:03.000
And the last step probably would be the&nbsp;
shipping, which obviously is something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.000 --> 00:16:08.480
that we take care of for DTC brands. Obviously,&nbsp;
there's always a mix between doing it by boat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.480 --> 00:16:14.960
by train, sometimes even if it's mainland&nbsp;
or close to China, and obviously by air.

00:16:14.960 --> 00:16:19.000
Definitely, these shipping options are always&nbsp;
there, and it really depends on what's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.000 --> 00:16:24.440
best fit. So definitely also something that&nbsp;
I would say is part of the sourcing process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.440 --> 00:16:31.520
of knowing how you want it to be shipped out.&nbsp;
That's definitely an aspect that is come into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:31.520 --> 00:16:35.480
play when it comes to sourcing from China&nbsp;
as well. You want to do a full container,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.480 --> 00:16:42.160
you want to do in batches, or you want part of it,&nbsp;
maybe fast by air to a location of your choice.

00:16:42.160 --> 00:16:47.480
It's definitely something that you need to take&nbsp;
care of as like a fifth step to consider. What I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.480 --> 00:16:53.000
would like to know actually from you is you've&nbsp;
dealt with so many factories and suppliers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.000 --> 00:16:57.080
and I always get the people that are dealing&nbsp;
with China, they're always wondering, like:

00:16:57.080 --> 00:17:04.640
‘Should I build a relationship? Should I visit the&nbsp;
factory? Should I be very strong on the price?’

00:17:04.640 --> 00:17:11.320
What is your take on what works best&nbsp;
when dealing with a factory or supplier?

00:17:11.320 --> 00:17:19.000
So it's an interesting question because&nbsp;
they're kind of all connected for us or for me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.000 --> 00:17:27.240
especially when I was based in China, it's all&nbsp;
about establishing a relationship with them.

00:17:27.240 --> 00:17:31.520
Factories really like it when you're&nbsp;
detail-oriented but flexible at the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:31.520 --> 00:17:40.040
time. They love to be face to face with you, and&nbsp;
I think when you are building a strong personal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:40.040 --> 00:17:47.160
relationship with them, you're going to be able&nbsp;
to get into those flexible pricing discussions or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.160 --> 00:17:52.440
lead time discussions, but more importantly,&nbsp;
with a personal relationship with them.

00:17:52.440 --> 00:17:56.760
By meeting with [factories] face to face, they're&nbsp;
going to start to give you preferential treatment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:56.760 --> 00:17:59.920
or they might even just give you better&nbsp;
terms. I have some clients that we were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:59.920 --> 00:18:05.960
working with them for a year and a half and we&nbsp;
get like net 15 terms with them and we pass that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:05.960 --> 00:18:09.360
down to the client because the client, that&nbsp;
frees up their cash flow to do more marketing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:09.360 --> 00:18:13.960
and they could scale their business. So I&nbsp;
think it all starts with the relationship.

00:18:13.960 --> 00:18:20.440
I think there's nothing wrong with&nbsp;
asking about price discounts. We do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:20.440 --> 00:18:25.160
it carefully because we don't want&nbsp;
them to think that we're just trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:25.160 --> 00:18:28.400
to get the cheapest possible&nbsp;
price or anything like that.

00:18:28.400 --> 00:18:31.840
But I'd say building a strong&nbsp;
relationship, being responsive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:31.840 --> 00:18:35.920
keeping them in check. At the same time,&nbsp;
they like to know that you're going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:35.920 --> 00:18:39.560
systematic about things because then they&nbsp;
know you're an easy person to deal with.

00:18:39.560 --> 00:18:43.560
When it comes to dealing with troubleshooting&nbsp;
issues, are you going to come up with solutions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:43.560 --> 00:18:50.200
with [the factory] to work through it? Sometimes&nbsp;
problems are not black and white. So being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:50.200 --> 00:18:55.600
flexible, having a good relationship, being&nbsp;
direct, is, I think, the most important part.

00:18:55.600 --> 00:19:00.120
Yeah, so great. Let me chime in on that. So back&nbsp;
when I was running my own brands and I'm actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.120 --> 00:19:05.800
working on some brands right now. I totally&nbsp;
agree that that face time—and it's not just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:05.800 --> 00:19:11.160
the FaceTime itself—but what that actually&nbsp;
enables that their relationship. Right at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:11.160 --> 00:19:16.920
the end of the day, it can be easy to think&nbsp;
that factories are just run like machines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:16.920 --> 00:19:20.360
especially if you've never visited one&nbsp;
before or never worked with one directly.

00:19:20.360 --> 00:19:25.400
They're not just run by machines. There may&nbsp;
be machinery, but a lot of the decisions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:25.400 --> 00:19:31.360
the production runs are still run by people. And&nbsp;
there's a lot of manual decisions and there's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:31.360 --> 00:19:37.200
lot of fluctuations and flexibility that can&nbsp;
actually be part of the process itself. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:37.200 --> 00:19:41.280
therefore there can be a lot of leeway&nbsp;
in really pretty much everything. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:41.280 --> 00:19:44.840
there's obviously limits, right? There's&nbsp;
limits on cost, there's limits on lead times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:44.840 --> 00:19:50.560
there's limits on capacity, but within those&nbsp;
limits, there can be a lot of flexibility.

00:19:50.560 --> 00:19:57.320
And this is where even if you as an end&nbsp;
client of a factory or sourcing agent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:57.320 --> 00:20:02.800
and you may not have the largest orders,&nbsp;
but you're the one that's actually there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:02.800 --> 00:20:08.760
and you're actually eating with them, drinking&nbsp;
with them, and building that relationship and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.760 --> 00:20:14.240
probably friendship with them as well, then&nbsp;
you're going to actually be able to get a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:14.240 --> 00:20:19.840
of things done that you normally would not&nbsp;
have, even if you have larger order volumes.

00:20:19.840 --> 00:20:23.680
And this is probably also a part&nbsp;
of just the Chinese business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:23.680 --> 00:20:29.920
culture or the East Asian business culture.
 
Whether it is good or bad, relationships are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:29.920 --> 00:20:35.960
very important in Chinese business in general.&nbsp;
Now that doesn't mean that you as a DTC brand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:35.960 --> 00:20:41.360
need to be at a factory all the time, but this&nbsp;
is one of the values of actually working with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:41.360 --> 00:20:47.520
sourcing agent, someone that can be in the middle,&nbsp;
whether it's extension of your team or an agency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:47.520 --> 00:20:53.160
that you can actually trust to represent you, to&nbsp;
build and maintain those relationships as well.

00:20:53.160 --> 00:20:59.200
100 percent agree with you. Every time I go&nbsp;
to China, it's just so many meetings with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:59.200 --> 00:21:05.200
factories and just getting that Facetime is so&nbsp;
valuable. And you build friendships. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:05.200 --> 00:21:10.240
that's the thing with doing business in China over&nbsp;
the years. And I'm sure both of you would agree&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:10.240 --> 00:21:15.680
with this. It's like when you're doing business&nbsp;
over there [in China], [it’s like] you're entering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:15.680 --> 00:21:21.480
their family because even a lot of factories that&nbsp;
we're all working with are family-run operations.

00:21:21.480 --> 00:21:24.560
So when you build that relationship with them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:24.560 --> 00:21:28.360
they're inviting you to their&nbsp;
hometown for spring festival.

00:21:28.360 --> 00:21:33.680
I can't begin to tell you how many spring&nbsp;
festivals I've had in the countryside with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:33.680 --> 00:21:38.680
like a factory owner, because we do a lot&nbsp;
of business together and they were like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:38.680 --> 00:21:43.680
hey, you should come to my hometown and&nbsp;
we should drink and you should meet all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:43.680 --> 00:21:49.240
my cousins and relatives and you're&nbsp;
really just like part of their life.

00:21:49.240 --> 00:21:53.080
And that is so different than the way&nbsp;
things are done in the United States and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:53.080 --> 00:21:58.720
Europe. And I think that's why I always&nbsp;
want to go back to China, just because I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:58.720 --> 00:22:03.160
really love how hospitable the culture&nbsp;
is there. So that would just be my final.

00:22:04.040 --> 00:22:09.160
Yeah, definitely, definitely recognize&nbsp;
that when you get into that circle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:09.160 --> 00:22:14.400
basically you're not going to leave that&nbsp;
circle again. You're part of it. It's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:14.400 --> 00:22:21.880
so much of the family is not a real actual&nbsp;
biological family. It's whoever touches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:21.880 --> 00:22:28.000
is influencing your well-being or growth. So&nbsp;
it's very powerful to be part of that circle.

00:22:28.000 --> 00:22:33.000
Definitely. When it comes to a lot of DTC brands&nbsp;
and a lot of people buying from China always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:33.000 --> 00:22:38.600
think, okay, the bigger the order, I need&nbsp;
to get lower prices as well. But obviously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:38.600 --> 00:22:43.440
there are also some issues that come with getting&nbsp;
bigger orders means you have to pay much more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:43.440 --> 00:22:50.200
upfront. How would you say to deal better with&nbsp;
these kinds of cash flow issues to overcome this?

00:22:50.200 --> 00:22:56.800
So since [we started] working with DTC brands,&nbsp;
I'd say we've been working heavily with them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:56.800 --> 00:23:03.280
over the last two and a half years now, where&nbsp;
we primarily just focus on working with them. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:03.280 --> 00:23:12.520
think it's all about leveraging that historical&nbsp;
sales data and what the future holds for their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:12.520 --> 00:23:19.480
month-to-month volumes. So that you're actually&nbsp;
changing the conversation about, not about, hey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:19.480 --> 00:23:24.160
I'm doing all this business with you, I need&nbsp;
to buy this much more so we can get to this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:24.160 --> 00:23:30.840
price. It's like: ‘no, how about over the next six&nbsp;
months I'm going to sell 200,000 units or more?’

00:23:30.840 --> 00:23:36.000
And if you're just continuing to produce&nbsp;
for us every month, can we just have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:36.000 --> 00:23:42.560
200,000 unit price? Of course, that's done over&nbsp;
dinner, meetings and stuff a lot of the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:42.560 --> 00:23:47.200
but that really is what it's about&nbsp;
because for my ecommerce clients,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:47.200 --> 00:23:52.600
mean ones that are doing fulfillment from China.&nbsp;
Try to keep a month or less of inventory and just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:52.600 --> 00:23:58.560
keep that factory churning product out and&nbsp;
getting it over to EcommOps, for example.

00:23:58.560 --> 00:24:02.040
And then we just forecast the purchasing. I mean,&nbsp;
of course, there's going to be some things that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:02.040 --> 00:24:06.960
we have to negotiate with the factory, like,&nbsp;
hey, can you keep more bottles on hand or,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:06.960 --> 00:24:11.680
okay, you kind of need us to produce a little bit&nbsp;
more. So can you just keep some inventory at your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:11.680 --> 00:24:16.080
factory? And then we'll just do faster shipping&nbsp;
times, because as long as you're not dealing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:16.080 --> 00:24:22.560
with products that have short expiration dates,&nbsp;
which is not very common, then you're able to work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:22.560 --> 00:24:26.520
with the factory as well. And the factories&nbsp;
we work with are so big, they have space.

00:24:26.520 --> 00:24:30.560
So I think it's just about trying&nbsp;
to find that happy medium between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:30.560 --> 00:24:35.800
fulfillment companies and the factory&nbsp;
and trying to produce smaller orders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:35.800 --> 00:24:40.040
but still get the high volume price&nbsp;
because you have the sales to back it up.

00:24:40.040 --> 00:24:46.920
Yeah, and I'll add to that as well. There&nbsp;
are a lot more variables to consider versus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:46.920 --> 00:24:54.320
just a simplified view of more ecos cheaper.&nbsp;
And usually, it takes longer as well. Right.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:54.320 --> 00:24:59.920
So when Greg is talking about maybe investing in&nbsp;
more bottles or other raw materials, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:59.920 --> 00:25:05.920
that could actually just be a fraction, like a&nbsp;
small fraction of total cost that either you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:05.920 --> 00:25:12.240
willing just to put a 10% extra deposit for those&nbsp;
materials, or the factory just absorbs it for you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:12.240 --> 00:25:18.800
Right. And the other consideration is how often&nbsp;
do you actually need that stock replenishment?

00:25:18.800 --> 00:25:23.680
So traditionally, if you're talking&nbsp;
about old-school traditional retail,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:23.680 --> 00:25:28.320
they're planning two to four&nbsp;
POs per year and massive POs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:28.960 --> 00:25:33.680
right? And it's supposed to last them for&nbsp;
at least 3, 4, 5, 6 months. And a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:33.680 --> 00:25:39.040
traditional factories, they're kind of used to&nbsp;
that model, so they kind of come with a standard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:39.040 --> 00:25:46.520
expectation of that. But that's actually not&nbsp;
exactly optimal for how a factory works as well.

00:25:47.600 --> 00:25:51.200
No factory wants to just produce four&nbsp;
times a year, right? I mean, Greg,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:51.200 --> 00:25:55.600
I'm sure you agree with that, right? No factory&nbsp;
wants to produce just a couple times per year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:55.600 --> 00:26:02.080
They have to crunch in order to do that. As a&nbsp;
matter of fact, they would actually prefer to.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:02.080 --> 00:26:06.280
What? The other extreme, actually, if they could&nbsp;
produce the exact same amount every single day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:06.280 --> 00:26:10.800
and not have to worry about their capacity&nbsp;
going up and down, that's the more ideal.

00:26:10.800 --> 00:26:15.000
And actually, this is closer&nbsp;
to that ideal is what we see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:15.000 --> 00:26:18.600
when you're able to compress your lead time.

00:26:18.600 --> 00:26:21.920
And we'll talk a little bit more about&nbsp;
that later. But that's one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:21.920 --> 00:26:25.640
biggest drivers that we've actually&nbsp;
seen. So either you're using air,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:25.640 --> 00:26:31.200
so you're having a way faster freight lead&nbsp;
time to your local fulfillment warehouse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:31.200 --> 00:26:39.360
or more and more brands are just shipping&nbsp;
directly from China and being able to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:39.360 --> 00:26:44.760
stock arrive at our Shenzhen warehouse in&nbsp;
like two days or one day or hours sometimes.

00:26:44.760 --> 00:26:48.200
And what that means is that as opposed&nbsp;
to being forced to holding three,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:48.200 --> 00:26:50.880
four months inventory at a time,&nbsp;
they could actually hold three,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:50.880 --> 00:26:54.720
four days of inventory at a time. And this&nbsp;
is kind of what Greg was talking about.

00:26:54.720 --> 00:26:59.440
You don’t work with a factory to&nbsp;
say ‘hey, I still want like two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:59.440 --> 00:27:04.600
months' worth of inventory. However, let's&nbsp;
figure out what your production schedule&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:04.600 --> 00:27:09.840
is.’ And as opposed to taking a month&nbsp;
and a half to produce a massive batch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:09.840 --> 00:27:14.440
does it actually make sense that you're actually&nbsp;
producing a much smaller amount every week,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:14.440 --> 00:27:19.920
every two weeks? And that might actually&nbsp;
be more optimal for the factory itself?

00:27:19.920 --> 00:27:23.440
Yeah. And this ties in back as&nbsp;
well. Not only just the planning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:23.440 --> 00:27:26.680
itself of when do you release the&nbsp;
goods, but also the packaging.

00:27:26.680 --> 00:27:30.240
Because if you're going to make the perfect&nbsp;
design of a product that is actually your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:30.240 --> 00:27:35.960
shipping air or it's not efficiently for an&nbsp;
air freight shipment. Because in the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:35.960 --> 00:27:41.160
everything is based on weight. So&nbsp;
that alignment on the actual supply,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:41.160 --> 00:27:46.680
as well as the efficiency and the&nbsp;
optimization on cost, placing a big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:46.680 --> 00:27:53.640
award, it doesn't necessarily create a healthier&nbsp;
cash flow situation, as well as the most optimal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:53.640 --> 00:27:58.120
supply chain that you could have, as well as&nbsp;
sometimes a discount can be in other areas.

00:27:58.960 --> 00:28:02.720
Just by being more efficient on your&nbsp;
packaging, on the actual weight of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:02.720 --> 00:28:07.520
product, as well as the timing on when&nbsp;
do you actually get shipments released&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:07.520 --> 00:28:18.480
and the payment terms. This is exactly what&nbsp;
you said, right? Everyone wins in that sense.

00:28:18.480 --> 00:28:24.200
Yeah. It's funny you say that, Simon, because we&nbsp;
were just having this conversation, you and me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:24.200 --> 00:28:31.520
last week, with one of our clients, right. How&nbsp;
we needed to figure out what is a cost saving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:31.520 --> 00:28:37.560
solution for the client by making the packaging&nbsp;
smaller so it could fit into mailboxes. That way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:37.560 --> 00:28:42.160
not only is he reducing some of his packaging&nbsp;
cost, but he's also reducing his shipping cost.

00:28:42.160 --> 00:28:47.400
And it's funny you bring it up, because we were&nbsp;
just discussing our plan of attack after Chinese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:47.400 --> 00:28:52.440
new year this year, taking care of that. So&nbsp;
you're right. And this is why working with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:52.440 --> 00:29:02.520
you guys is just so key. Because you guys are able&nbsp;
to advise us as the sourcing agent dimensions and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:02.520 --> 00:29:07.240
how to make certain weights work just so that it's&nbsp;
more optimal for the client on the shipping side.

00:29:07.240 --> 00:29:12.920
So I'll chime in there as well, because&nbsp;
I have a lot of omnichannel experience.

00:29:12.920 --> 00:29:19.120
So thinking about retail displays, for example,&nbsp;
and this is where the packaging needs to be really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:19.120 --> 00:29:23.240
considered, how you're actually going to sell&nbsp;
it and how you're going to physically fulfill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:23.240 --> 00:29:28.040
it. A lot of factories for certain product&nbsp;
lines, skincare is a good example, actually.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:28.720 --> 00:29:31.640
They're more optimized for retail display.

00:29:31.640 --> 00:29:37.320
I was actually just at a skincare factory that, I&nbsp;
mean, I was with your team, Greg, at the skincare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:37.320 --> 00:29:44.840
factory in Suzhou, and we were asking, so how&nbsp;
are you going, like what is the outside box?

00:29:44.840 --> 00:29:49.440
We're looking at bottles and tubes and they&nbsp;
were like, we don't even think about that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:50.080 --> 00:29:56.120
Our clients don't even ask about what the box&nbsp;
actually looks like. And I realized though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:56.120 --> 00:30:02.520
because the bulk of their clients are actually&nbsp;
primarily producing for wholesale retail and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:02.520 --> 00:30:05.920
where the box doesn't really matter as&nbsp;
much as the display of the tube itself.

00:30:05.920 --> 00:30:13.280
Right. However, let's take skincare as an&nbsp;
example. As more and more skincare brands go DTC,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:13.280 --> 00:30:18.400
you're going to actually require to consider&nbsp;
what is the right packaging actually for DTC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:18.400 --> 00:30:23.680
shipping. And if you don't have a hard&nbsp;
enough box that looks also nice enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:23.680 --> 00:30:27.400
it's going to break in shipping and it's&nbsp;
going to have a poor unboxing experience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:27.400 --> 00:30:31.320
which is especially critical&nbsp;
for something like skincare.

00:30:31.320 --> 00:30:34.880
Especially if you're selling at more of&nbsp;
a medium to higher price range, they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:34.880 --> 00:30:40.840
going to be higher customer expectations&nbsp;
of how that package arrives as well.

00:30:40.840 --> 00:30:45.200
So definitely make sure that you're also&nbsp;
thinking about the end customer experience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:45.200 --> 00:30:48.840
what physical fulfillment method&nbsp;
that you're actually using,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:48.840 --> 00:30:52.560
and at least consider what type of&nbsp;
packaging is actually more ideal for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:52.560 --> 00:30:58.000
your fulfillment strategy and not just&nbsp;
what seems to be standard in the market.

00:30:58.000 --> 00:31:04.080
Totally agree all about the unboxing experience&nbsp;
when it comes to being an ecommerce business.

00:31:04.080 --> 00:31:10.080
Yeah, so we're really teaming up together,&nbsp;
EcommOps and Kanary Solutions. Actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:10.080 --> 00:31:15.120
what we're doing is building an ecosystem around,&nbsp;
at least in our case, around the fulfillment.

00:31:15.120 --> 00:31:20.720
So you will see many more of our partners on the&nbsp;
